{"id":"adapt-the-challenge","title":"Adapt the challenge","minutes":6,"tracks":["coach"],"summary":"Choose one change for one child, then see what happens.","sections":[{"heading":"See the difference","html":"<p>Two children can play the same game and experience a very different challenge. One may lose the ball before they have time to look up. Another may keep scoring without needing to make a new decision. Your role is to notice the difference and adjust what each child is being asked to do.</p><p>Your guides call extra support a superpower and extra challenge a constraint. The purpose is to keep the child involved with a challenge they can explore. These are temporary responses, rather than labels for children.</p>"},{"heading":"Give the change a purpose","html":"<p>Start with Sharks &amp; Surfers. Surfers have a ball and try to score; sharks try to steal and keep it. Three seconds of shark possession earns a point. The original card suggests a freeze pinnie for support and scoring from the ocean only for extra challenge. Read the original page alongside the written resource card.</p><p>Before changing anything, describe what you hope the child will experience: time to look, an opportunity to protect the ball, or a reason to find a different shooting position. A rule is useful only if its effect serves the child and the game.</p>"},{"heading":"Change one thing","html":"<p>In a separate 1v1 example, a player repeatedly loses the ball immediately after a restart. The guide's restart headstart gives that child three seconds of free play. Explain the change briefly and watch whether the child now has an opportunity to look, decide and act.</p><p>For a player needing more challenge, a marked scoring area can invite a new decision about where to shoot. This is a proposed application; choose its position for the child and your space. Avoid stacking several rules before you know what one has changed.</p>"},{"heading":"Watch and adjust","html":"<p>Notice involvement and choices as well as goals. Does the child attempt something, want another turn and understand the rule? Recognise intent even if the attempt fails. Ask how the change felt. Keep, reduce, replace or remove it after observation. One successful round is useful feedback about today; it does not establish retained learning over time.</p>"}],"mediaIds":["sharks","spectrum"],"quiz":[{"id":"adapt-the-challenge-1","prompt":"A player loses possession immediately at every restart. Which option directly offers time to decide?","options":["A four-second scoring limit.","A three-second restart headstart.","A narrower goal."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The headstart temporarily delays pressure. Check whether it creates a useful decision for this child."},{"id":"adapt-the-challenge-2","prompt":"You changed numbers, scoring and field size at once. The game improved. What can you conclude?","options":["One rule definitely caused it.","The whole approach is scientifically validated.","The combined changes looked useful today; you cannot isolate which change helped."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Multiple changes obscure the cause. Describe what you saw without claiming proof."},{"id":"adapt-the-challenge-3","prompt":"A support rule now makes the game too easy for its recipient. What next?","options":["Reassess with the child and reduce or replace the support.","Keep it forever.","Remove the child."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The coaching spectrum is fluid. Respond to changing involvement and challenge."}],"takeaway":"Name the intended effect, make one change, observe and decide what to do next.","sourceRefs":[{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":25},{"sourceId":"guide-2","page":29},{"sourceId":"guide-8","page":31}]}
